\def\title{CACHE}

@i ../../types.gw

@* Introduction.
The OpenResty version of mpresty kept the bodies of remote graphics sources
in an nginx shared dictionary, declared in \.{mpresty.conf} as
$$\hbox{\.{lua\_shared\_dict mpresty\_cache 10m;}}$$
A shared dictionary is a curious beast. It lives in shared memory so that
several nginx worker processes can see it, and it is bounded in bytes because
shared memory cannot grow. When I ported mpresty to \GO/ in August 2026
neither property was worth keeping: there is one process, and its heap grows
as it likes. What remains is the essence of the thing, a table that forgets
what nobody has asked for lately.

So this package is a plain {\it least recently used\/} cache of strings,
bounded by the number of entries rather than their size, and guarded by a
single mutex so that the goroutines rendering a page may share it. The
renderer keys it by the URL of a remote source; nothing else in mpresty uses
it.

@ The whole package fits in one file. Its skeleton lists the parts in the
order I explain them.
@c
// Package cache is the LRU cache mpresty keeps remote graphics sources in.
package cache

import (
	"container/list"
	"sync"
)

@<The cache type@>
@<Functions@>

@* The cache.
The classic way to build an LRU cache is a hash table whose values point into
a doubly linked list. The list keeps the entries in order of use, most recent
at the front; the table finds an entry's place in the list in constant time.
A hit moves the entry to the front, and when the cache overflows the entry at
the back is the one nobody has missed. The standard package |list| provides
the list, and a \GO/ map provides the table.

Each list element carries an |entry|, which remembers its own key as well as
its value. The key looks redundant, since the map already knows it, but
eviction starts from the list and must then find its way back into the map.
@<The cache type@>=
// DefaultCapacity is the number of entries kept before eviction.
const DefaultCapacity = 4096

type entry struct {
	key string
	val string
}

@ The cache itself is the list, the map, the capacity, and the mutex that
protects all three. Every method takes the mutex for its whole body; the
work done under it is a map lookup and a few pointer swaps, far too little
to be worth anything finer.
@<The cache type@>=
// Cache is a safe-for-concurrent-use LRU of strings.
type Cache struct {
	mu    sync.Mutex
	cap   int
	ll    *list.List
	items map[string]*list.Element
}

@ A capacity of zero or less asks for the default. That convention lets the
command line say \.{-cache 0} to mean ``whatever is sensible,'' and lets the
renderer call |New(0)| when nobody gave it a cache at all.
@<Functions@>=
// New returns a cache holding at most capacity entries. A capacity of zero
// or less means DefaultCapacity.
func New(capacity int) *Cache {
	if capacity <= 0 {
		capacity = DefaultCapacity
	}
	return &Cache{
		cap:   capacity,
		ll:    list.New(),
		items: make(map[string]*list.Element, capacity),
	}
}

@ A lookup is also a use, so a hit moves its entry to the front of the list.
That is why |Get| needs the full mutex and not merely a read lock: in an LRU
cache even reading writes.
@<Functions@>=
// Get returns the value stored under key, if any.
func (c *Cache) Get(key string) (string, bool) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	el, ok := c.items[key]
	if !ok {
		return "", false
	}
	c.ll.MoveToFront(el)
	return el.Value.(*entry).val, true
}

@ Storing under a key we already hold replaces the value and counts as a
use. Otherwise the new entry goes to the front, and the back of the list
pays for it. The loop could be an |if|, since a single insertion can push
the cache over its capacity by one entry at most; I wrote it as a loop
because then the invariant $|ll.Len()|\le|cap|$ is evident from this
section alone.
@<Functions@>=
// Set stores a value, evicting the least recently used entry if full.
func (c *Cache) Set(key, val string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if el, ok := c.items[key]; ok {
		c.ll.MoveToFront(el)
		el.Value.(*entry).val = val
		return
	}
	c.items[key] = c.ll.PushFront(&entry{key: key, val: val})
	for c.ll.Len() > c.cap {
		if el := c.ll.Back(); el != nil {
			c.ll.Remove(el)
			delete(c.items, el.Value.(*entry).key)
		}
	}
}

@ The size is reported for the tests' sake.
@<Functions@>=
// Len reports how many entries are held.
func (c *Cache) Len() int {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.ll.Len()
}

@* Tests.
\.{GTANGLE} writes the tests to \.{cache\_test.go}, beside the package. The
first test plays out the textbook scenario with a cache of two entries:
reading \.{a} makes it the most recent, so storing \.{c} must evict \.{b}.
@(cache_test.go@>=
package cache

import (
	"strconv"
	"sync"
	"testing"
)

func TestEvictsLeastRecentlyUsed(t *testing.T) {
	c := New(2)
	c.Set("a", "1")
	c.Set("b", "2")
	if _, ok := c.Get("a"); !ok {
		t.Fatal("a missing")
	}
	c.Set("c", "3")
	if _, ok := c.Get("b"); ok {
		t.Error("b should have been evicted")
	}
	if v, ok := c.Get("a"); !ok || v != "1" {
		t.Errorf("a = %q, %v", v, ok)
	}
	if n := c.Len(); n != 2 {
		t.Errorf("Len = %d, want 2", n)
	}
}

@ The second test asserts nothing by itself. It hammers the cache from
thirty-two goroutines at once, and earns its keep under \.{go test -race},
which would complain if any method touched the list or the map outside the
mutex.
@(cache_test.go@>=
func TestConcurrentUse(t *testing.T) {
	c := New(64)
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := range 32 {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			k := strconv.Itoa(i)
			c.Set(k, k)
			c.Get(k)
		}()
	}
	wg.Wait()
}

@* Index.
